
[38;5;231m⏺[39m [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m has no scripts, no dependencies, and no test runner; there's no linter or build step. [38;5;153msrc/[39m is CommonJS
  Node and unrelated to the login path — [38;5;153mapp.js[39m is plain browser globals loaded by a [38;5;153m<script>[39m tag. So the storage code
  has to work as a browser global with no module system, and there is currently no way to test it. I'll fold a tooling
  question into the design section.

  Next clarifying question.

  [1mThe fork:[0m what [38;5;153mlogin[39m receives when [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m isn't usable. This is not a corner case here — the app is a bare
  [38;5;153mindex.html[39m, so it will often be opened over [38;5;153mfile://[39m, and private-browsing modes and storage-disabled settings both
  make [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m access throw rather than return null. Whatever I pick becomes the value analysis sees.

  - [1mIn-memory fallback[0m — generate a UUID for the page session and use it. [38;5;153mlogin[39m always gets a well-formed ID, nothing
    branches. Cost: the ID [3mlooks[0m persistent but isn't, so any "how many attempts from returning browsers" question
    silently counts these as new browsers every time. The corruption is invisible.
  - [1mExplicit [38;5;153mnull[0m — pass [38;5;153mnull[39m and let consumers see that no durable ID was available. Honest data; costs a null branch
    in [38;5;153mlogin[39m and in anything downstream. Hard to change later once consumers assume non-null.
  - [1mBlock login[0m — don't call [38;5;153mlogin[39m without an ID. I'd rule this out: never fail authentication because a tracking
    identifier is unavailable.

  My recommendation is [1mexplicit [38;5;153mnull[0m. The whole point of the persistent choice was linking attempts across page loads;
  an in-memory ID quietly claims to do that and doesn't, and silently wrong analytics is worse than a visible gap. It
  also keeps the privacy posture clean — no ID gets invented for users who've deliberately disabled storage.
